          "type": "text",
          "text": "---\nname: continual-arc\ndescription: Solve Continual-ARC grids by applying saved task transforms, learning from demos, and submitting verified outputs.\n---\n\n# Continual-ARC\n\n1. At instance start, inspect `programs/<task id>.py` in the specified workspace. If present, run its `transform(test_grid)`, validate dimensions and cell values, and submit that exact output before any other action; do not infer it's invalid from visual inspection. If absent, request a demo.\n2. Transcribe each complete demo pair exactly and keep it for regression checks. Infer the rule from the specific structures and compare alternative hypotheses against all pairs; request another demo only when it can distinguish plausible rules. For palette transformations, infer the complete mapping of every present color—including background, marker, and output colors—from the demos; do not assume foreground identity or reuse a mapping from a previous task. Test the candidate mapping on every cell of every demo before accepting it. If multiple demos show a rectangular frame with missing edge cells recolored, test the foreground bounding box's perimeter as the candidate rule: recolor only background cells on its min/max row or column, never the interior, and accept it only if it exactly reproduces every demo.\n3. After a wrong submission, don't resubmit the same output or make intuition-only edits. Revisit every demo, and run the current transform on each exact demo input; compare the program-produced grids cell-for-cell with the expected outputs before using its test output again. A plausible rule is not validated until these checks pass. Then revise the rule to account for mismatches, requesting a discriminating demo when evidence remains ambiguous. After every edit, rerun all demo regressions. If the demos do not establish the rule (including when the cap is exhausted), keep uncertainty explicit and don't call a heuristic verified. For ray/extension rules, check which object sends the continuation and where it stops; don't extend based only on foreground occupancy or add unverified companion fills.\n4. Run the transform on the full test grid; assert dimensions, rectangularity, and cell range before submitting exactly the program-produced grid. Do not hand-transcribe a grid for submission: a transcription mistake can make a valid ARC answer malformed. If validation fails, fix the input/output serialization and rerun the program; if no transform reproduces every available demo, leave the task file unchanged. Save a validated replacement only when the instance ends as directed, and confirm the write.\n5. On every turn, emit only one action JSON object as the entire reply, without commentary or markdown. Request demos when evidence is needed; submit only grids produced by the task program; use `finish` only after the instance is over. Never add explanatory prose around the action JSON. For binary recoloring tasks, determine the mapping from each demo's actual input/output colors; do not assume a fixed `5 -> x`, marker-to-zero, or palette-specific rule.\n"
        }
